content.text:
I implemented `LRUCache` in `src/lru.js` and `node --test` passes all 3 tests.

It uses one private `Map`. A `Map` keeps keys in the order they were added, so the oldest key is always the least recently used one.
- **Constructor:** throws `RangeError` unless the capacity is a positive whole number of type Number. Zero, negatives, fractions, `Infinity`, `NaN`, strings, missing values, `null`, `new Number(2)` and `2n` are all rejected. The `capacity` property the stub already set is still there.
- **`get`:** if the key exists, it removes and re-adds the entry so it becomes most recently used, even when the stored value is `undefined`. Otherwise it returns `undefined`.
- **`set`:** updating an existing key moves it to most recent without evicting anything. Adding a new key when the cache is full first removes the oldest entry. It returns the cache, so calls can be chained.
- **`has`, `delete`, `size`:** these pass straight through to the `Map`, so `has` doesn't change the order and `delete` leaves the order of the other entries unchanged.

The existing 3 tests don't cover most of the edge cases. A separate one-off script, now discarded, checked all the rejected capacities and these cases:
- refreshing an entry whose value is `undefined`;
- updating a key without evicting another or growing the size;
- `has` not changing the order;
- `NaN`, `-0` and object keys behaving as they do in a `Map`;
- the order after a delete;
- `delete` returning true, then false for the same key;
- separate caches not affecting each other.

All passed after I fixed a wrong expectation in my own script; the cache code didn't change.

No dependencies were added, and `test/` and `package.json` are untouched.

